/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 41-45
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:41, name:"The Treachery of Ishmael", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 41:2",verse:"Then arose Ishmael the son of Nethaniah, and the ten men that were with him, and smote Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan with the sword, and slew him, whom the king of Babylon had made governor over the land."}, facts:["Did you know Gedaliah had been warned that Ishmael meant to kill him, but he refused to believe the warning (Jeremiah 40:14-16)?","Did you know the pit where Ishmael dumped the bodies was an old wartime cistern built by King Asa generations earlier, when he feared Baasha king of Israel (Jeremiah 41:9)?","Did you know Ishmael's massacre was ordered from abroad? He fled straight to the Ammonites, whose king had sent him (Jeremiah 40:14; 41:15)."], questions:[
{q:"Who was Ishmael's father?", options:["Nethaniah","Kareah","Ahikam","Shaphan"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:2",verse:"Then arose Ishmael the son of Nethaniah, and the ten men that were with him, and smote Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan with the sword, and slew him, whom the king of Babylon had made governor over the land.", insight:"Ishmael was 'of the seed royal,' a prince turned assassin; position never excuses treachery."},
{q:"How many men came with Ishmael to Mizpah?", options:["Ten men","Eight men","Twelve men","Seventy men"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:1",verse:"Now it came to pass in the seventh month, that Ishmael the son of Nethaniah the son of Elishama, of the seed royal, and the princes of the king, even ten men with him, came unto Gedaliah the son of Ahikam to Mizpah; and there they did eat bread together in Mizpah.", insight:"Ten men at the table, ten swords in the dark; beware of company that comes too quietly."},
{q:"What were they doing together when Ishmael rose against Gedaliah?", options:["They were eating bread together","They were offering a sacrifice","They were sleeping in the courtyard","They were counting the stores"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:1",verse:"Now it came to pass in the seventh month, that Ishmael the son of Nethaniah the son of Elishama, of the seed royal, and the princes of the king, even ten men with him, came unto Gedaliah the son of Ahikam to Mizpah; and there they did eat bread together in Mizpah.", insight:"He struck at the table, where trust is most sacred; shared bread should never hide a sword."},
{q:"Whom had the king of Babylon made governor over the land?", options:["Gedaliah the son of Ahikam","Ishmael the son of Nethaniah","Johanan the son of Kareah","Zedekiah the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:2",verse:"Then arose Ishmael the son of Nethaniah, and the ten men that were with him, and smote Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan with the sword, and slew him, whom the king of Babylon had made governor over the land.", insight:"Gedaliah ruled by Babylon's appointment; killing him was rebellion wearing the face of patriotism."},
{q:"Besides the Jews, whom did Ishmael also slay at Mizpah?", options:["The Chaldeans that were found there, and the men of war","The priests of the house of the LORD","The elders of Samaria","The captives from Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:3",verse:"Ishmael also slew all the Jews that were with him, even with Gedaliah, at Mizpah, and the Chaldeans that were found there, and the men of war.", insight:"He killed Chaldeans too; rage aimed at one man rarely stops where it starts."},
{q:"From which three places did the fourscore mourners come?", options:["Shechem, Shiloh, and Samaria","Hebron, Bethel, and Ai","Dan, Bethel, and Gilgal","Joppa, Ashkelon, and Gaza"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:5",verse:"That there came certain from Shechem, from Shiloh, and from Samaria, even fourscore men, having their beards shaven, and their clothes rent, and having cut themselves, with offerings and incense in their hand, to bring them to the house of the LORD.", insight:"They came from the north with offerings for a ruined house; devotion outlived the temple's walls."},
{q:"What were the mourners carrying to the house of the LORD?", options:["Offerings and incense","Wheat and barley","Gold and silver","Firstfruits of oil"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:5",verse:"That there came certain from Shechem, from Shiloh, and from Samaria, even fourscore men, having their beards shaven, and their clothes rent, and having cut themselves, with offerings and incense in their hand, to bring them to the house of the LORD.", insight:"They brought offerings to a house that lay in ruins; worship does not wait for perfect circumstances."},
{q:"How did Ishmael greet the mourners as he went out to meet them?", options:["Weeping all along as he went","Singing a psalm of comfort","Blowing a trumpet of welcome","Leading them with armed men"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:6",verse:"And Ishmael the son of Nethaniah went forth from Mizpah to meet them, weeping all along as he went: and it came to pass, as he met them, he said unto them, Come to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam.", insight:"He wept all the way to meet them; tears can be the mask of a murderer."},
{q:"What did Ishmael say to the mourners?", options:["Come to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam","Come, rest in Mizpah","Follow me to the Ammonites","Lay down your offerings here"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:6",verse:"And Ishmael the son of Nethaniah went forth from Mizpah to meet them, weeping all along as he went: and it came to pass, as he met them, he said unto them, Come to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam.", insight:"He lured them with the name of the man he had killed; evil borrows good names to open doors."},
{q:"Where did Ishmael cast the bodies of the slain mourners?", options:["Into the midst of the pit","Into the river Kidron","Into the valley of Hinnom","Outside the city gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:7",verse:"And it was so, when they came into the midst of the city, that Ishmael the son of Nethaniah slew them, and cast them into the midst of the pit, he, and the men that were with him.", insight:"The pit swallowed the worshippers; God sees every grave that cruelty digs in the dark."},
{q:"Why did Ishmael spare ten of the mourners?", options:["They promised him treasures of wheat, barley, oil, and honey hidden in the field","They were princes of the royal house","They begged for mercy on their knees","They were kin to Gedaliah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:8",verse:"But ten men were found among them that said unto Ishmael, Slay us not: for we have treasures in the field, of wheat, and of barley, and of oil, and of honey. So he forbare, and slew them not among their brethren.", insight:"Greed stayed his sword where mercy could not; even killers answer to their appetites."},
{q:"Which king had made the pit where Ishmael cast the bodies?", options:["Asa, for fear of Baasha king of Israel","Solomon, for his storehouses","Hezekiah, for the siege","Jehoshaphat, for his army"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:9",verse:"Now the pit wherein Ishmael had cast all the dead bodies of the men, whom he had slain because of Gedaliah, was it which Asa the king had made for fear of Baasha king of Israel: and Ishmael the son of Nethaniah filled it with them that were slain.", insight:"A cistern built for war became a grave for worshippers; old defenses cannot stop new evils."},
{q:"Whom did Ishmael carry away captive from Mizpah?", options:["All the residue of the people, including the king's daughters","Only the mighty men of war","The priests and the Levites","The Chaldean soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:10",verse:"Then Ishmael carried away captive all the residue of the people that were in Mizpah, even the king’s daughters, and all the people that remained in Mizpah, whom Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard had committed to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam: and Ishmael the son of Nethaniah carried them away captive, and departed to go over to the Ammonites.", insight:"He stole the people Gedaliah had protected, even the king's daughters; cruelty collects the helpless first."},
{q:"To whom did Ishmael depart to go with his captives?", options:["The Ammonites","The Moabites","The Edomites","The Philistines"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:10",verse:"Then Ishmael carried away captive all the residue of the people that were in Mizpah, even the king’s daughters, and all the people that remained in Mizpah, whom Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard had committed to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam: and Ishmael the son of Nethaniah carried them away captive, and departed to go over to the Ammonites.", insight:"He ran to the Ammonites, back to the master who sent him; every hired sword returns to its payer."},
{q:"Who heard of Ishmael's evil and rose to fight him?", options:["Johanan the son of Kareah, and the captains","Baruch the son of Neriah","Jeremiah the prophet","Ebed-melech the Ethiopian"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:11",verse:"But when Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the captains of the forces that were with him, heard of all the evil that Ishmael the son of Nethaniah had done,", insight:"Johanan heard and acted; when evil is done, silence is not neutrality."},
{q:"Where did Johanan's forces find Ishmael?", options:["By the great waters that are in Gibeon","At the gates of Mizpah","In the plains of Bethlehem","At the fords of Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:12",verse:"Then they took all the men, and went to fight with Ishmael the son of Nethaniah, and found him by the great waters that are in Gibeon.", insight:"They caught him by the great waters of Gibeon; the fleeing wicked are never as far as they think."},
{q:"How did the captives react when they saw Johanan?", options:["They were glad, and returned unto him","They fled into the hills","They wept and would not move","They fought against him"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:13",verse:"Now it came to pass, that when all the people which were with Ishmael saw Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the captains of the forces that were with him, then they were glad.", insight:"They were glad at the sight of rescue; God's deliverance turns captives into a celebrating people."},
{q:"How many men escaped with Ishmael to the Ammonites?", options:["Eight men","Ten men","Seven men","Twelve men"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:15",verse:"But Ishmael the son of Nethaniah escaped from Johanan with eight men, and went to the Ammonites.", insight:"Eight men fled with him into the night; the mighty murderer ended as a fugitive."},
{q:"Where did the rescued people dwell before entering Egypt?", options:["The habitation of Chimham, which is by Bethlehem","The city of Mizpah","The plains of Jericho","The gates of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:17",verse:"And they departed, and dwelt in the habitation of Chimham, which is by Bethlehem, to go to enter into Egypt,", insight:"They camped near Bethlehem, the city of David; even in fear, God kept them near His promises."},
{q:"Why did the people plan to enter into Egypt?", options:["Because of the Chaldeans, for they were afraid of them","Because Pharaoh had invited them","Because of the famine in Judah","Because Ishmael had fled there"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 41:18",verse:"Because of the Chaldeans: for they were afraid of them, because Ishmael the son of Nethaniah had slain Gedaliah the son of Ahikam, whom the king of Babylon made governor in the land.", insight:"They feared Babylon more than they trusted God; fear makes poor travel plans."}
]},
{n:42, name:"A Warning Against Egypt", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 42:11",verse:"Be not afraid of the king of Babylon, of whom ye are afraid; be not afraid of him, saith the LORD: for I am with you to save you, and to deliver you from his hand."}, facts:["Did you know God waited ten days before answering their prayer, and the answer was not the one they wanted to hear (Jeremiah 42:7)?","Did you know the people swore to obey God's answer 'whether it be good, or whether it be evil,' then refused it outright (Jeremiah 42:6; 43:4)?","Did you know God's promise 'I am with you to save you' was given to those who stayed put in obedience, not to those who ran to Egypt, showing obedience is safer than escape (Jeremiah 42:11)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who came near to Jeremiah the prophet?", options:["All the captains of the forces, Johanan, Jezaniah, and all the people from the least to the greatest","Only the elders of Mizpah","The king's daughters alone","The priests of the house of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:1",verse:"Then all the captains of the forces, and Johanan the son of Kareah, and Jezaniah the son of Hoshaiah, and all the people from the least even unto the greatest, came near,", insight:"From the least to the greatest they came; trouble drives all ranks to seek a word from God."},
{q:"What did the people ask Jeremiah to do for them?", options:["Pray for the remnant unto the LORD","Lead them into Egypt","Hide them from the Chaldeans","Offer a sacrifice for them"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:2",verse:"And said unto Jeremiah the prophet, Let, we beseech thee, our supplication be accepted before thee, and pray for us unto the LORD thy God, even for all this remnant; (for we are left but a few of many, as thine eyes do behold us:)", insight:"They asked for prayer, the right request; the trouble was they had already decided the answer."},
{q:"How did the people describe themselves to Jeremiah?", options:["We are left but a few of many","We are strong and ready for war","We are rich in flocks and herds","We are strangers in the land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:2",verse:"And said unto Jeremiah the prophet, Let, we beseech thee, our supplication be accepted before thee, and pray for us unto the LORD thy God, even for all this remnant; (for we are left but a few of many, as thine eyes do behold us:)", insight:"'A few of many'; judgment had thinned them, and they knew it."},
{q:"What did they want the LORD to show them?", options:["The way wherein they may walk, and the thing that they may do","The treasures hidden in Mizpah","The day of Babylon's fall","The road into Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:3",verse:"That the LORD thy God may shew us the way wherein we may walk, and the thing that we may do.", insight:"They asked for the way and the work; asking God's direction is wise, ignoring it is ruin."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah promise the people?", options:["I will pray, and declare whatsoever the LORD answers, keeping nothing back","I will go with you to Egypt","I will hide you from Babylon","I will speak only words of comfort"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:4",verse:"Then Jeremiah the prophet said unto them, I have heard you; behold, I will pray unto the LORD your God according to your words; and it shall come to pass, that whatsoever thing the LORD shall answer you, I will declare it unto you; I will keep nothing back from you.", insight:"'I will keep nothing back'; a true prophet delivers the whole answer, not the popular part."},
{q:"What vow did the people make to Jeremiah?", options:["The LORD be a true and faithful witness between us, if we do not obey all He sends","We will build a new temple","We will offer ten thousand sacrifices","We will never leave Judah again"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:5",verse:"Then they said to Jeremiah, The LORD be a true and faithful witness between us, if we do not even according to all things for the which the LORD thy God shall send thee to us.", insight:"They called God as witness to their promise; vows made in haste still bind the soul."},
{q:"What did the people pledge, whether it be good or evil?", options:["We will obey the voice of the LORD our God","We will stay in Judah forever","We will pay tribute to Babylon","We will follow Johanan's counsel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:6",verse:"Whether it be good, or whether it be evil, we will obey the voice of the LORD our God, to whom we send thee; that it may be well with us, when we obey the voice of the LORD our God.", insight:"'Whether it be good, or whether it be evil, we will obey'; they promised before they heard, then broke it after."},
{q:"How long was it before the word of the LORD came to Jeremiah?", options:["After ten days","After three days","After forty days","The very same hour"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:7",verse:"And it came to pass after ten days, that the word of the LORD came unto Jeremiah.", insight:"Ten days of waiting before God spoke; heaven's silence is not heaven's absence."},
{q:"What did God promise if they would still abide in the land?", options:["I will build you, and not pull you down; I will plant you, and not pluck you up","I will make you rulers over Egypt","I will give you the spoils of Babylon","I will hide you in the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:10",verse:"If ye will still abide in this land, then will I build you, and not pull you down, and I will plant you, and not pluck you up: for I repent me of the evil that I have done unto you.", insight:"Build, not pull down; plant, not pluck up. God's heart toward the obedient is always constructive."},
{q:"Why did God say He would relent toward them?", options:["For I repent me of the evil that I have done unto you","Because they were many","Because Pharaoh had repented","Because the temple was rebuilt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:10",verse:"If ye will still abide in this land, then will I build you, and not pull you down, and I will plant you, and not pluck you up: for I repent me of the evil that I have done unto you.", insight:"God repents of the evil He brings, never of the good He promises; His mercy outruns His judgment."},
{q:"Of whom did God tell them not to be afraid?", options:["The king of Babylon, of whom ye are afraid","The king of Egypt","The Ammonites","The Chaldean captains"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:11",verse:"Be not afraid of the king of Babylon, of whom ye are afraid; be not afraid of him, saith the LORD: for I am with you to save you, and to deliver you from his hand.", insight:"'Be not afraid of the king of Babylon'; the fear they fled from was the very thing God said to face."},
{q:"What reason did God give for not fearing the king of Babylon?", options:["For I am with you to save you, and to deliver you from his hand","For Babylon is already falling","For Egypt will protect you","For the king has repented"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:11",verse:"Be not afraid of the king of Babylon, of whom ye are afraid; be not afraid of him, saith the LORD: for I am with you to save you, and to deliver you from his hand.", insight:"'I am with you to save you'; God's presence is the only safe place, and it travels with the obedient."},
{q:"What mercies did God promise them?", options:["He would show them mercies, that the king may have mercy and cause them to return to their own land","He would give them chariots and horses","He would make them priests","He would send them to a new land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:12",verse:"And I will shew mercies unto you, that he may have mercy upon you, and cause you to return to your own land.", insight:"God can soften a conqueror's heart; mercy from enemies is His specialty."},
{q:"Where did the people say they would go instead?", options:["Into the land of Egypt","Back to Babylon","Into the land of Moab","Over to the Ammonites"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:14",verse:"Saying, No; but we will go into the land of Egypt, where we shall see no war, nor hear the sound of the trumpet, nor have hunger of bread; and there will we dwell:", insight:"Egypt, the old house of bondage; the heart always wants to run back to what it knows."},
{q:"What did they hope to find in Egypt?", options:["No war, nor the sound of the trumpet, nor hunger of bread","Gold and silver","A new king","The ark of the covenant"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:14",verse:"Saying, No; but we will go into the land of Egypt, where we shall see no war, nor hear the sound of the trumpet, nor have hunger of bread; and there will we dwell:", insight:"No war, no trumpet, no hunger; they wanted comfort, not God, and comfort without God never lasts."},
{q:"What would overtake them in the land of Egypt?", options:["The sword which they feared, and the famine which they dreaded","Peace and plenty","The favor of Pharaoh","A new temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:16",verse:"Then it shall come to pass, that the sword, which ye feared, shall overtake you there in the land of Egypt, and the famine, whereof ye were afraid, shall follow close after you there in Egypt; and there ye shall die.", insight:"The sword they fled would chase them; you cannot outrun God's judgment by changing countries."},
{q:"How many of those who fled to Egypt would escape?", options:["None of them shall remain or escape","Half of them","Only the children","Only the women"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:17",verse:"So shall it be with all the men that set their faces to go into Egypt to sojourn there; they shall die by the sword, by the famine, and by the pestilence: and none of them shall remain or escape from the evil that I will bring upon them.", insight:"'None shall remain or escape'; disobedience leaves no back door."},
{q:"What would the fugitives become in Egypt?", options:["An execration, and an astonishment, and a curse, and a reproach","A blessing to the nations","Kings and princes","A mighty army"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:18",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; As mine anger and my fury hath been poured forth upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem; so shall my fury be poured forth upon you, when ye shall enter into Egypt: and ye shall be an execration, and an astonishment, and a curse, and a reproach; and ye shall see this place no more.", insight:"A curse and a reproach; running from God turns a people into a warning."},
{q:"What plain command did the LORD give concerning Egypt?", options:["Go ye not into Egypt","Go quickly into Egypt","Send spies into Egypt","Trade with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:19",verse:"The LORD hath said concerning you, O ye remnant of Judah; Go ye not into Egypt: know certainly that I have admonished you this day.", insight:"'Go ye not into Egypt'; some of God's clearest words are two-letter commands."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah say they had done in their hearts when they sent him to pray?", options:["They dissembled in their hearts","They believed with all their hearts","They repented in their hearts","They rejoiced in their hearts"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 42:20",verse:"For ye dissembled in your hearts, when ye sent me unto the LORD your God, saying, Pray for us unto the LORD our God; and according unto all that the LORD our God shall say, so declare unto us, and we will do it.", insight:"They dissembled, pretending to seek God while hiding their plan; God is not fooled by pious asking."}
]},
{n:43, name:"The Stones at Tahpanhes", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 43:10",verse:"And say unto them, Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will send and take Nebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon, my servant, and will set his throne upon these stones that I have hid; and he shall spread his royal pavilion over them."}, facts:["Did you know Tahpanhes, where the remnant fled for safety, became the very stage where God announced Egypt's coming conquest (Jeremiah 43:7-10)?","Did you know God called Nebuchadrezzar 'my servant' even as the Jews were fleeing to Egypt to escape him (Jeremiah 43:10)?","Did you know Beth-shemesh means 'house of the sun,' the Egyptian city of sun worship whose images God said would be broken (Jeremiah 43:13)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who accused Jeremiah of speaking falsely?", options:["Azariah the son of Hoshaiah, Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the proud men","Baruch the son of Neriah","The king's daughters","The priests of Tahpanhes"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:2",verse:"Then spake Azariah the son of Hoshaiah, and Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the proud men, saying unto Jeremiah, Thou speakest falsely: the LORD our God hath not sent thee to say, Go not into Egypt to sojourn there:", insight:"'All the proud men' led the charge; pride always shoots the messenger first."},
{q:"What did they say Jeremiah spoke?", options:["Falsely, for the LORD hath not sent thee to say, Go not into Egypt","Wisely, but too harshly","Truly, but too late","Softly, and without power"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:2",verse:"Then spake Azariah the son of Hoshaiah, and Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the proud men, saying unto Jeremiah, Thou speakest falsely: the LORD our God hath not sent thee to say, Go not into Egypt to sojourn there:", insight:"They called God's word false because it crossed their plans; unbelief renames truth to suit itself."},
{q:"Whom did they blame for setting Jeremiah against them?", options:["Baruch the son of Neriah","Ebed-melech","The Chaldeans","The elders of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:3",verse:"But Baruch the son of Neriah setteth thee on against us, for to deliver us into the hand of the Chaldeans, that they might put us to death, and carry us away captives into Babylon.", insight:"They blamed Baruch, the quiet scribe; when men reject God, they attack His servants."},
{q:"What did they claim Baruch intended to do?", options:["Deliver them into the hand of the Chaldeans, to be slain or carried captive","Steal the temple treasures","Lead them into the wilderness","Crown himself king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:3",verse:"But Baruch the son of Neriah setteth thee on against us, for to deliver us into the hand of the Chaldeans, that they might put us to death, and carry us away captives into Babylon.", insight:"They invented a conspiracy to dodge a command; suspicion is often disobedience in disguise."},
{q:"What did Johanan and all the people do about the LORD's voice?", options:["They obeyed not the voice of the LORD, to dwell in the land of Judah","They obeyed it gladly","They asked for a second sign","They waited forty days"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:4",verse:"So Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the captains of the forces, and all the people, obeyed not the voice of the LORD, to dwell in the land of Judah.", insight:"'They obeyed not'; after all the praying and promising, they simply refused."},
{q:"Who took all the remnant of Judah into Egypt?", options:["Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the captains of the forces","Azariah alone","The king's daughters","The Chaldean guard"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:5",verse:"But Johanan the son of Kareah, and all the captains of the forces, took all the remnant of Judah, that were returned from all nations, whither they had been driven, to dwell in the land of Judah;", insight:"Johanan led the exodus of disobedience; one strong leader can drag a whole remnant the wrong way."},
{q:"Which groups were dragged into Egypt with the remnant?", options:["Men, women, children, the king's daughters, Jeremiah, and Baruch","Only the mighty men of war","Only the priests and Levites","Only the elders"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:6",verse:"Even men, and women, and children, and the king’s daughters, and every person that Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard had left with Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan, and Jeremiah the prophet, and Baruch the son of Neriah.", insight:"Even Jeremiah and Baruch were carried along; the righteous sometimes suffer the journeys of the stubborn."},
{q:"To which city in Egypt did they come?", options:["Tahpanhes","Migdol","Noph","Pathros"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:7",verse:"So they came into the land of Egypt: for they obeyed not the voice of the LORD: thus came they even to Tahpanhes.", insight:"Tahpanhes, a border fortress of Egypt; they ran to the very edge of God's patience."},
{q:"What did the LORD tell Jeremiah to take in his hand?", options:["Great stones","A yoke of wood","A linen girdle","A potter's vessel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:9",verse:"Take great stones in thine hand, and hide them in the clay in the brickkiln, which is at the entry of Pharaoh’s house in Tahpanhes, in the sight of the men of Judah;", insight:"Great stones in the prophet's hand; God preaches with objects when words are refused."},
{q:"Where was Jeremiah to hide the stones?", options:["In the clay in the brickkiln, at the entry of Pharaoh's house in Tahpanhes","In the temple court at Jerusalem","Under the great waters of Gibeon","In the cave at Mizpah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:9",verse:"Take great stones in thine hand, and hide them in the clay in the brickkiln, which is at the entry of Pharaoh’s house in Tahpanhes, in the sight of the men of Judah;", insight:"At the entry of Pharaoh's house; God plants His warning on the enemy's own doorstep."},
{q:"Where was Jeremiah when the word of the LORD came to him about Egypt?", options:["In Tahpanhes","In Jerusalem","In Mizpah","In Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:8",verse:"Then came the word of the LORD unto Jeremiah in Tahpanhes, saying,", insight:"God spoke to Jeremiah in Tahpanhes itself; His word follows the disobedient to the very place they ran to."},
{q:"Whom would God send against Egypt?", options:["Nebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon, my servant","Pharaoh-hophra","The king of Assyria","The Medes and Persians"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:10",verse:"And say unto them, Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will send and take Nebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon, my servant, and will set his throne upon these stones that I have hid; and he shall spread his royal pavilion over them.", insight:"God called Babylon's king 'my servant'; the hammer of judgment belongs to God, not to chance."},
{q:"Where would Nebuchadrezzar set his throne?", options:["Upon these stones that I have hid","In the temple at Jerusalem","By the river Euphrates","In the palace at Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:10",verse:"And say unto them, Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will send and take Nebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon, my servant, and will set his throne upon these stones that I have hid; and he shall spread his royal pavilion over them.", insight:"His throne on the hidden stones; God had marked the spot before the conqueror arrived."},
{q:"When did Azariah and Johanan accuse Jeremiah of speaking falsely?", options:["When he had made an end of speaking all the words of the LORD their God","Before he had spoken a single word","During the siege of Jerusalem","After the fall of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:1",verse:"And it came to pass, that when Jeremiah had made an end of speaking unto all the people all the words of the LORD their God, for which the LORD their God had sent him to them, even all these words,", insight:"They waited until he finished speaking, then rejected it all; hearing is easy, obeying is the hard part."},
{q:"What would happen when he came to the land of Egypt?", options:["He shall smite it: death to death, captivity to captivity, sword to sword","He shall make peace with Pharaoh","He shall build new cities","He shall free the captives"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:11",verse:"And when he cometh, he shall smite the land of Egypt, and deliver such as are for death to death; and such as are for captivity to captivity; and such as are for the sword to the sword.", insight:"Death, captivity, sword; the refuge they ran to would become their ruin."},
{q:"What would God kindle in Egypt?", options:["A fire in the houses of the gods of Egypt","A lamp in Pharaoh's palace","A beacon on the border","A feast in Tahpanhes"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:12",verse:"And I will kindle a fire in the houses of the gods of Egypt; and he shall burn them, and carry them away captives: and he shall array himself with the land of Egypt, as a shepherd putteth on his garment; and he shall go forth from thence in peace.", insight:"Fire in the houses of Egypt's gods; the idols they trusted would burn first."},
{q:"How would the conqueror array himself with the land of Egypt?", options:["As a shepherd putteth on his garment","As a king with his crown","As a warrior with his sword","As a priest with his ephod"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:12",verse:"And I will kindle a fire in the houses of the gods of Egypt; and he shall burn them, and carry them away captives: and he shall array himself with the land of Egypt, as a shepherd putteth on his garment; and he shall go forth from thence in peace.", insight:"Like a shepherd putting on a garment, easy and unhurried; God's judgments fit Him without strain."},
{q:"According to Jeremiah 43:7, why had they come into the land of Egypt?", options:["For they obeyed not the voice of the LORD","Because Pharaoh had invited them","Because of the famine in Judah","Because Ishmael had fled there"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:7",verse:"So they came into the land of Egypt: for they obeyed not the voice of the LORD: thus came they even to Tahpanhes.", insight:"Scripture names their disobedience plainly; they came to Egypt because they would not obey."},
{q:"What would he break in the land of Egypt?", options:["The images of Beth-shemesh","The walls of Tahpanhes","The ships of Pharaoh","The crowns of the priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:13",verse:"He shall break also the images of Beth-shemesh, that is in the land of Egypt; and the houses of the gods of the Egyptians shall he burn with fire.", insight:"The sun god's images would shatter; no idol survives the day God moves against it."},
{q:"What would he do to the houses of the gods of the Egyptians?", options:["Burn them with fire","Carry them to Babylon","Fill them with treasure","Leave them untouched"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 43:13",verse:"He shall break also the images of Beth-shemesh, that is in the land of Egypt; and the houses of the gods of the Egyptians shall he burn with fire.", insight:"Burned with fire; the gods of Egypt would end as ashes, like every god men make."}
]},
{n:44, name:"The Queen of Heaven", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 44:16",verse:"As for the word that thou hast spoken unto us in the name of the LORD, we will not hearken unto thee."}, facts:["Did you know the 'queen of heaven' was a foreign goddess, likely from Assyria and Babylon, whom the people credited for their years of plenty instead of the LORD (Jeremiah 44:17-18)?","Did you know this crowd answered a prophet with the blunt words 'we will not hearken unto thee,' one of the most defiant replies in all of Scripture (Jeremiah 44:16)?","Did you know Pharaoh-hophra, the king in whose land they trusted, was later handed to his enemies exactly as God promised (Jeremiah 44:30)?"], questions:[
{q:"To which Jews did this word of the LORD come?", options:["Those dwelling in Egypt at Migdol, Tahpanhes, Noph, and in the country of Pathros","Those still in Jerusalem","Those carried to Babylon","Those in the land of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:1",verse:"The word that came to Jeremiah concerning all the Jews which dwell in the land of Egypt, which dwell at Migdol, and at Tahpanhes, and at Noph, and in the country of Pathros, saying,", insight:"God followed the fugitives into Egypt with His word; running from Him only lengthens the sermon."},
{q:"What had the people seen with their own eyes?", options:["All the evil God had brought upon Jerusalem and the cities of Judah","The rebuilding of the temple","The fall of Babylon","The return of the captives"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Ye have seen all the evil that I have brought upon Jerusalem, and upon all the cities of Judah; and, behold, this day they are a desolation, and no man dwelleth therein,", insight:"They had seen Jerusalem's ruin; eyewitnesses to judgment should be the slowest to repeat the sin."},
{q:"What was the condition of Jerusalem and the cities of Judah?", options:["A desolation, and no man dwelleth therein","Full of people and rejoicing","Rebuilt stronger than before","Ruled by a new king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Ye have seen all the evil that I have brought upon Jerusalem, and upon all the cities of Judah; and, behold, this day they are a desolation, and no man dwelleth therein,", insight:"Desolate, without inhabitant; sin empties cities faster than any army."},
{q:"Why had God brought this evil upon them?", options:["Because of their wickedness, burning incense and serving other gods","Because they were too few","Because Babylon was too strong","Because the prophets were silent"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:3",verse:"Because of their wickedness which they have committed to provoke me to anger, in that they went to burn incense, and to serve other gods, whom they knew not, neither they, ye, nor your fathers.", insight:"Incense to other gods provoked Him; worship given to idols is theft from God."},
{q:"How had God sent His prophets to warn them?", options:["Rising early and sending them","Once every seven years","Only in visions","Through angels alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:4",verse:"Howbeit I sent unto you all my servants the prophets, rising early and sending them, saying, Oh, do not this abominable thing that I hate.", insight:"'Rising early and sending'; God warns diligently, like a father up before dawn for his children."},
{q:"What did God call the idolatry He hated?", options:["This abominable thing that I hate","A small mistake","A custom of the fathers","A harmless pastime"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:4",verse:"Howbeit I sent unto you all my servants the prophets, rising early and sending them, saying, Oh, do not this abominable thing that I hate.", insight:"'This abominable thing that I hate'; God does not merely dislike idolatry, He hates it."},
{q:"What did the people refuse to do?", options:["Turn from their wickedness, to burn no incense unto other gods","Leave the land of Judah","Pay tribute to Babylon","Listen to the priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:5",verse:"But they hearkened not, nor inclined their ear to turn from their wickedness, to burn no incense unto other gods.", insight:"They would not turn; the tragedy was never a lack of warning, but a lack of turning."},
{q:"What would happen to the remnant that set their faces toward Egypt?", options:["They shall all be consumed, and fall in the land of Egypt","They shall prosper and multiply","They shall return in triumph","They shall be protected by Pharaoh"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:12",verse:"And I will take the remnant of Judah, that have set their faces to go into the land of Egypt to sojourn there, and they shall all be consumed, and fall in the land of Egypt; they shall even be consumed by the sword and by the famine: they shall die, from the least even unto the greatest, by the sword and by the famine: and they shall be an execration, and an astonishment, and a curse, and a reproach.", insight:"Consumed in the very land they chose for safety; the wrong refuge becomes a grave."},
{q:"By what would they be consumed?", options:["By the sword and by the famine","By plague alone","By old age","By wild beasts"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:12",verse:"And I will take the remnant of Judah, that have set their faces to go into the land of Egypt to sojourn there, and they shall all be consumed, and fall in the land of Egypt; they shall even be consumed by the sword and by the famine: they shall die, from the least even unto the greatest, by the sword and by the famine: and they shall be an execration, and an astonishment, and a curse, and a reproach.", insight:"Sword and famine, the old judgments; God repeats His warnings in deeds when words fail."},
{q:"How many of the remnant in Egypt would return to Judah?", options:["None shall return but such as shall escape","All of them in triumph","Half of them","Only the priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:14",verse:"So that none of the remnant of Judah, which are gone into the land of Egypt to sojourn there, shall escape or remain, that they should return into the land of Judah, to the which they have a desire to return to dwell there: for none shall return but such as shall escape.", insight:"Only a few would escape; mercy leaves a remnant, but disobedience costs the crowd."},
{q:"Who answered Jeremiah in Pathros?", options:["The men whose wives had burned incense, all the women, and a great multitude","The captains of the forces","The king's daughters","The priests of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:15",verse:"Then all the men which knew that their wives had burned incense unto other gods, and all the women that stood by, a great multitude, even all the people that dwelt in the land of Egypt, in Pathros, answered Jeremiah, saying,", insight:"A great multitude answered, husbands and wives together; sin loves company, and finds it."},
{q:"What was their blunt reply to Jeremiah?", options:["As for the word thou hast spoken, we will not hearken unto thee","We will obey with all our hearts","Give us seven days to consider","We will ask the LORD again"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:16",verse:"As for the word that thou hast spoken unto us in the name of the LORD, we will not hearken unto thee.", insight:"'We will not hearken'; defiance dressed in plain words is still defiance."},
{q:"To whom did they vow to burn incense?", options:["The queen of heaven","The LORD of hosts","The gods of the Chaldeans","The sun and the moon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:17",verse:"But we will certainly do whatsoever thing goeth forth out of our own mouth, to burn incense unto the queen of heaven, and to pour out drink offerings unto her, as we have done, we, and our fathers, our kings, and our princes, in the cities of Judah, and in the streets of Jerusalem: for then had we plenty of victuals, and were well, and saw no evil.", insight:"The queen of heaven, a foreign goddess; when God's people borrow gods, they always borrow trouble."},
{q:"Why did they prefer their old idolatry?", options:["For then had we plenty of victuals, and were well, and saw no evil","For the LORD had forsaken them","For Pharaoh commanded it","For the prophets were dead"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:17",verse:"But we will certainly do whatsoever thing goeth forth out of our own mouth, to burn incense unto the queen of heaven, and to pour out drink offerings unto her, as we have done, we, and our fathers, our kings, and our princes, in the cities of Judah, and in the streets of Jerusalem: for then had we plenty of victuals, and were well, and saw no evil.", insight:"'Then had we plenty'; they credited idols for God's blessings, the oldest mistake in worship."},
{q:"What happened since they left off burning incense to the queen of heaven?", options:["They wanted all things, and were consumed by sword and famine","They grew rich and strong","They found true peace","They returned to Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:18",verse:"But since we left off to burn incense to the queen of heaven, and to pour out drink offerings unto her, we have wanted all things, and have been consumed by the sword and by the famine.", insight:"They read their troubles backwards, blaming obedience for what disobedience caused."},
{q:"What did the women make for the queen of heaven?", options:["Cakes to worship her","Golden calves","Silver altars","Linen veils"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:19",verse:"And when we burned incense to the queen of heaven, and poured out drink offerings unto her, did we make her cakes to worship her, and pour out drink offerings unto her, without our men?", insight:"Cakes baked for a goddess; devotion is real even when its object is false, and that is the sorrow of it."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah ask them to remember about their incense in Judah?", options:["Did not the LORD remember it, and came it not into His mind?","Had the kings approved it?","Were the cakes well baked?","Did the neighbors join in?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:21",verse:"The incense that ye burned in the cities of Judah, and in the streets of Jerusalem, ye, and your fathers, your kings, and your princes, and the people of the land, did not the LORD remember them, and came it not into his mind?", insight:"God remembered every wisp of that incense; nothing offered to idols escapes His notice."},
{q:"Why could the LORD no longer bear their doings?", options:["Because of the evil of their doings, and the abominations they committed","Because He was too weak","Because Babylon threatened Him","Because the people were too many"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:22",verse:"So that the LORD could no longer bear, because of the evil of your doings, and because of the abominations which ye have committed; therefore is your land a desolation, and an astonishment, and a curse, without an inhabitant, as at this day.", insight:"'The LORD could no longer bear'; even divine patience has its limit, and they had reached it."},
{q:"What had their land become?", options:["A desolation, an astonishment, a curse, without an inhabitant","A fruitful garden","A mighty kingdom","A place of pilgrimage"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:22",verse:"So that the LORD could no longer bear, because of the evil of your doings, and because of the abominations which ye have committed; therefore is your land a desolation, and an astonishment, and a curse, without an inhabitant, as at this day.", insight:"Desolation without inhabitant; the land kept the Sabbath rest they had refused it."},
{q:"What did God swear by His great name?", options:["That His name shall no more be named in the mouth of any man of Judah in all Egypt","That He would destroy the temple","That He would forget Judah forever","That He would bless Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 44:26",verse:"Therefore hear ye the word of the LORD, all Judah that dwell in the land of Egypt; Behold, I have sworn by my great name, saith the LORD, that my name shall no more be named in the mouth of any man of Judah in all the land of Egypt, saying, The Lord GOD liveth.", insight:"His name silenced in their mouths; those who will not praise Him in truth will not speak Him at all."}
]},
{n:45, name:"Baruch's Promise", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 45:5",verse:"And seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not: for, behold, I will bring evil upon all flesh, saith the LORD: but thy life will I give unto thee for a prey in all places whither thou goest."}, facts:["Did you know Baruch, exhausted from writing Jeremiah's prophecies of doom, received the only personal promise in the whole book: his life would be spared wherever he went (Jeremiah 45:5)?"], questions:[
{q:"To whom did Jeremiah speak this word?", options:["Baruch the son of Neriah","Johanan the son of Kareah","Azariah the son of Hoshaiah","Ebed-melech the Ethiopian"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel, unto thee, O Baruch;", insight:"'Unto thee, O Baruch'; after chapters of judgment on nations, God speaks tenderly to one weary scribe."},
{q:"What had Baruch written at the mouth of Jeremiah?", options:["These words in a book","A letter to Pharaoh","A song of praise","The law of Moses"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:1",verse:"The word that Jeremiah the prophet spake unto Baruch the son of Neriah, when he had written these words in a book at the mouth of Jeremiah, in the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, saying,", insight:"Baruch wrote what Jeremiah spoke; faithful scribes carry the weight of words not their own."},
{q:"In which year did Baruch write these words?", options:["In the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah","In the first year of Zedekiah","In the eleventh year of Josiah","In the year Jerusalem fell"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:1",verse:"The word that Jeremiah the prophet spake unto Baruch the son of Neriah, when he had written these words in a book at the mouth of Jeremiah, in the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, saying,", insight:"The fourth year of Jehoiakim; God dates His comforts as carefully as His judgments."},
{q:"How is the LORD named in His word to Baruch?", options:["The LORD, the God of Israel","The LORD of hosts","The God of Abraham","The Holy One of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel, unto thee, O Baruch;", insight:"'The God of Israel'; even in personal sorrow, God comes as the covenant God of His people."},
{q:"What was Baruch's cry?", options:["Woe is me now! for the LORD hath added grief to my sorrow","Blessed be the LORD forever","How long, O LORD?","The LORD is my strength"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:3",verse:"Thou didst say, Woe is me now! for the LORD hath added grief to my sorrow; I fainted in my sighing, and I find no rest.", insight:"'Woe is me'; God lets His servants say it out loud before He answers."},
{q:"How did Baruch describe his weariness?", options:["I fainted in my sighing, and I find no rest","I am strong and of good courage","I have eaten and am satisfied","I sing in the night"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:3",verse:"Thou didst say, Woe is me now! for the LORD hath added grief to my sorrow; I fainted in my sighing, and I find no rest.", insight:"'I find no rest'; the man who wrote everyone else's doom was breaking under its weight."},
{q:"What would God do to that which He had built?", options:["Break it down","Build it higher","Leave it standing","Give it to Baruch"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:4",verse:"Thus shalt thou say unto him, The LORD saith thus; Behold, that which I have built will I break down, and that which I have planted I will pluck up, even this whole land.", insight:"What He built, He would break down; God's honesty about judgment comes before His comfort."},
{q:"What would God do to that which He had planted?", options:["Pluck it up, even this whole land","Water it with rain","Prune it carefully","Transplant it to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:4",verse:"Thus shalt thou say unto him, The LORD saith thus; Behold, that which I have built will I break down, and that which I have planted I will pluck up, even this whole land.", insight:"Plucked up, the whole land; when God uproots, no one is exempt, not even the faithful scribe's country."},
{q:"What searching question did God put to Baruch?", options:["Seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not","Wilt thou be My prophet?","Dost thou love Me more than these?","Wherefore didst thou doubt?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:5",verse:"And seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not: for, behold, I will bring evil upon all flesh, saith the LORD: but thy life will I give unto thee for a prey in all places whither thou goest.", insight:"'Seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not'; ambition shrinks beside a collapsing nation."},
{q:"What promise did God give to Baruch?", options:["Thy life will I give unto thee for a prey in all places whither thou goest","Thou shalt be made a prince","Thou shalt see Babylon fall","Thou shalt return to Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 45:5",verse:"And seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not: for, behold, I will bring evil upon all flesh, saith the LORD: but thy life will I give unto thee for a prey in all places whither thou goest.", insight:"His life as a prey rescued from the hunter; in a day of ruin, God saved the scribe's life as his portion."}
]}
]);
